WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.093
A sequence of functions changes an entire graph at each index

00:00:05.093 --> 00:00:11.920
At any fixed input below one, repeated multiplication by that input drives the value toward zero

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.333
At input one, every function still equals one.

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:21.993
This is pointwise convergence: each fixed input gets its own eventual control

00:00:21.993 --> 00:00:24.673
But the limit jumps at one

00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:34.500
The increasingly steep graphs near that endpoint show why controlling each location separately is not the same as controlling every location simultaneously.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.400
Compare x divided by n on the same interval

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.400
The error from zero is at most one over n for every input at once

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.200
A single index therefore works across the whole interval, establishing uniform convergence.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.252
Uniform limits of continuous functions remain continuous

00:00:55.252 --> 00:01:00.077
Pointwise limits need not, as the power example demonstrates

00:01:00.077 --> 00:01:11.333
Exchanging limits with other operations requires an appropriate theorem; a visually settling sequence alone does not justify exchanging derivatives or integrals.
